# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
BLOCKS = {
    "base": [
        ("Why a lanai floor skips the rock base entirely",
         "<p>A screen enclosure or lanai floor around a {city} pool is almost always bare concrete, so there is no soil underneath to build a normal base from. Instead of two to four inches of washed, open-graded crushed rock, the crew glues a thin drainage underlay straight to the slab, and that underlay carries splash and rinse water sideways toward whatever drain the deck already uses. An open border between the same cage and a side fence still gets the standard rock base, graded one to two percent, because that strip sits on native sand able to take water straight down. Two areas on one {city} property, built on completely different foundations underneath the same green surface.</p>"),
        ("Grading a deck border toward the drain it already has",
         "<p>Most yards only need their base graded one to two percent away from the house, but a pool-deck border in {city} gets aimed at the deck's existing low point instead, since runoff already sheds off the cage and coping onto that strip during every storm. Washed, open-graded crushed rock underneath keeps that added flow moving rather than binding into a crust at the transition where concrete meets soil. Skipping this and grading only away from the foundation leaves water pooling along the coping edge, which is exactly where bare feet cross most often on the way in and out of a {city} cage.</p>"),
        ("What actually holds up a turf ribbon between pavers?",
         "<p>A narrow strip of turf set into a paver joint near the pool does not sit on a rock base at all; the pavers themselves carry the load, and the turf gets a thin leveling layer of sand in the joint rather than the two to four inches used everywhere else on a {city} lot. That thin joint still has to drain rather than trap water against the paver edges, so the sand underneath needs to stay loose enough to percolate, the same principle behind leaving native soil uncompacted under a full-size base. A joint packed too tight holds water at the surface just as a bad rock base would.</p>"),
        ("Why the ground near the pump pad stays wetter than the rest of the yard",
         "<p>The equipment pad next to a pool, where the pump and filter sit, sheds backwash and drips more consistently than any other spot on a {city} lot, and turf bordering that pad needs the same washed rock depth and undisturbed native sand as anywhere else to keep that extra moisture moving down instead of sitting at the surface. Compacting the soil under that section too hard, which is easy to do when equipment traffic has already packed it down over the years, works against the percolation the state's turf standard requires. A border here often needs a fresh pass of loosening before rock goes down.</p>"),
        ("Why washed rock matters most where concrete meets soil",
         "<p>Rainfall that hits a pool deck's concrete has nowhere to go but sideways, onto whatever soil border sits at the cage's edge, so a {city} lot's washed, open-graded crushed rock does more work at that transition than it does in the middle of an open lawn. Unwashed fill would let fines settle and bind right where the extra volume arrives first, turning a narrow strip into the one part of the yard that stays soggy after every storm. The state's rule that subgrade material be washed before installation exists for exactly this kind of concentrated flow, not just ordinary rain falling evenly across a yard.</p>"),
    ],
    "product": [
        ("Infill choice for turf that sits right at the waterline",
         "<p>Turf bordering a {city} pool still falls under the same single-family infill rule as the rest of the lot: clean silica sand, zeolite, or a coated sand with a non-toxic coating, never a rubber or synthetic fill outside the footprint of playground equipment. Coated sand is a reasonable pick for a strip that takes regular splash, since the coating resists bacteria growth on the grains themselves rather than relying on the sand alone. Plain silica works fine further back from the coping where splash rarely reaches. Either choice still goes in at roughly one to two pounds per square foot, brushed evenly through the pile.</p>"),
        ("Why a shaded lanai floor tends to outlast an open deck border",
         "<p>Synthetic turf generally lasts ten to twenty years depending on UV stabilization, traffic, and infill, and a glued lanai floor under screen mesh in {city} usually lands toward the higher end of that window, since the mesh cuts a meaningful amount of direct UV exposure compared with an open border baking in full sun all day. Foot traffic works the other direction: a well-used lanai floor near the door sees more steps over a season than a border strip along a fence line ever does. Which factor wins out depends on the household, but shade alone does not guarantee the longest possible lifespan.</p>"),
        ("What does a small deck border cost, worked out?",
         "<p>Installed turf runs eight to eighteen dollars a square foot, typically ten to sixteen, and price does not change by {city} ZIP code, only by access, base condition, and drainage. Say a homeowner wants a 250 sq ft strip between the pool cage and a side fence turfed rather than reseeded: at the typical range, that job runs somewhere between roughly twenty-five hundred and four thousand dollars, before accounting for anything unusual about the site. A quote well outside that range for a plain open border, high or low, is worth asking about specifically, since the underlying material and labor do not vary by neighborhood.</p>"),
        ("The backing still has to drain even glued to a slab",
         "<p>A permeable backing built to pass more than thirty inches of water an hour matters on a {city} lanai floor for a different reason than it does on an open lawn: since the concrete slab underneath cannot absorb anything itself, the backing and the drainage underlay together are doing the entire job that native sand and rock handle everywhere else on the property. A slower or partly blocked backing on a glued floor shows up faster than the same flaw would on soil, because there is no secondary path for water to disappear into once it clears the turf.</p>"),
        ("Brushing infill up to a hard glued edge instead of a soft one",
         "<p>Infill along a nailed perimeter on soil settles and shifts slightly as the ground below moves with moisture, but infill against a glued edge on a {city} pool deck sits against a perimeter that never moves at all, since it is bonded straight to concrete or set into a coping line. That fixed edge means infill brushed at the standard one to two pounds per square foot holds its finished line more predictably along a lanai floor than along a border with a soft, nailed perimeter, where a slightly heavier hand near the edge helps offset the natural settling a soil-backed perimeter still does over its first season.</p>"),
    ],
    "process": [
        ("Letting the adhesive cure before the pool goes back into use",
         "<p>Glued seams and perimeter adhesive on a {city} lanai floor need time to set before anyone walks the surface, and that cure window matters more here than on a soil job because a pool deck stays humid and occasionally wet even when it is not actively raining. Foot traffic across a seam before the adhesive has fully bonded can shift the join enough to open a gap later, and a splash from the pool itself while the glue is still curing does the same thing rainfall would to an unfinished soil job. Scheduling the last coat early in the day gives it hours of dry cure before evening pool use resumes.</p>"),
        ("Why does coping get a different anchor than a fence line?",
         "<p>The state's turf standard requires edges anchored against wind and flooding, but how that anchoring happens changes at a {city} pool: along a fence line, the perimeter still takes nails or a bender-board edge into soil the way any residential border would, while along the coping itself, the edge bonds with adhesive into the hard trim rather than being pinned into anything soft. Mixing the two methods up, nailing into coping or trying to glue a soft fence-line edge, leaves a section that either cannot hold or cracks the material it is anchored into within a season.</p>"),
        ("Building a border and a glued floor as one sequenced job",
         "<p>When a {city} property gets both an open deck border and a glued lanai floor at the same visit, the rock base for the border goes in and compacts first, before the slab gets pressure washed for the glue-down section, so dust and loose fines from the soil work do not land on a clean, prepped slab. Compacting the border early also gives that section time to settle while the crew finishes prepping and gluing the floor, rather than rushing both areas to be ready on the same afternoon. The two areas finish on the same day even though they are built in a different order.</p>"),
        ("Confirming the drain point before infill goes down",
         "<p>Once the drainage underlay is glued to a {city} lanai floor but before any infill goes in, the crew runs water across the surface to confirm it actually reaches the deck's existing drain rather than pooling in a low spot the underlay created by accident. Catching a flat or misdirected section at this stage means lifting a corner of underlay and reworking it, which is straightforward before infill is down and considerably more disruptive after. This check takes a few minutes and is the reason a finished lanai floor rarely surprises anyone with standing water months later.</p>"),
        ("Capping the sprinkler zone that used to reach the deck border",
         "<p>Any irrigation head that used to water the strip along a {city} pool cage gets capped at the valve during base work, the same step any residential turf conversion goes through, since the state's rule against watering synthetic turf with an in-ground system applies right up to the cage wall. From that point on, the hose bib already mounted near most pool equipment pads becomes the rinse source for that section, which is convenient since it is usually closer to the border than the nearest hose bib elsewhere on the property. Nobody has to run a hose across the whole yard just to reach it.</p>"),
    ],
    "risks": [
        ("Bare feet and hot turf between the loungers and the water",
         "<p>Full summer sun pushes turf surface temperature to 120 to 150°F, and the stretch of turf between a set of pool loungers and the water in {city} sees more bare feet crossing it in an afternoon than almost anywhere else on the property. A hose rinse drops that temperature 30 to 50 degrees within minutes, which matters more here than on a side yard nobody walks barefoot across. Timing pool time for morning or late afternoon in peak summer, or rinsing the crossing points before anyone gets in the water, does more for comfort than any product choice does on its own.</p>"),
        ("What a wall of pool-cage sliders can do to the turf below",
         "<p>Large sliding glass doors are common on {city} pool homes, and a low-emissivity coating on that much glass can reflect enough concentrated sunlight to soften turf directly in its path, since polyethylene blades start to give around 175 to 200°F under that kind of focused heat. The damage shows up as a scorched patch shaped by the reflection angle rather than by foot traffic, which is often the first clue it is glare and not wear. Checking a west- or south-facing wall of sliders before laying turf close to it avoids relocating a section later.</p>"),
        ("Keeping a shaded pool corner outside an oak's root zone",
         "<p>A mature oak shading part of a {city} pool deck sounds like the ideal spot for turf that stays cooler through the afternoon, but the drip-line rule applies here exactly as it does anywhere else on the lot: no turf inside the canopy's root zone without a certified arborist's letter confirming the install will not harm the tree. Digging even a shallow lanai-floor edge or a border's rock base near exposed surface roots close to the trunk can do real damage before anyone notices. Getting that letter before committing to a shaded layout is the only way to use the space legally.</p>"),
        ("Does the pool itself count toward the ten-foot water setback?",
         "<p>A {city} lot backing onto a retention pond, lake, or canal still has to keep turf at least ten feet from that water's edge unless a seawall or bulkhead already separates the two, and that rule reaches right up to the property line behind a pool cage even though the pool inside the cage does not count as a waterbody at all. Turf can run straight to the coping with no setback of its own. Confusing the two, and assuming a rear setback applies just because there is water somewhere on the property, can shrink a planned layout for no reason.</p>"),
        ("Why a slab that never fully dries is a warning sign",
         "<p>A glued lanai floor built without enough drainage underlay traps splash and rinse water against the concrete instead of routing it to the deck drain, and the first sign is usually a slab that stays damp longer after use than it should, not a visible puddle anyone notices right away in {city}'s humidity. Adhesive bonds weaker against a slab that never fully dries between uses, so a corner that lifts months after installation often traces back to that trapped moisture rather than a bad glue job on its own. Fixing it after the fact means pulling turf, not just adding infill.</p>"),
    ],
    "care": [
        ("Two reasons to rinse a pool border more than once a week in summer",
         "<p>Hosing down turf along a {city} pool deck does two jobs at once: it clears chlorine or salt splash and pollen before either dries into the fibers, and it drops surface temperature 30 to 50 degrees ahead of an afternoon swim. Rainy-season storms between June and September handle some of that rinsing on their own, but the dry stretches on either side of summer leave splash as the only source of moisture on the turf, so a hose rinse earns its place in the routine year-round rather than just during the hottest weeks. Keeping shock and acid containers off the turf's edge avoids undoing the rinse entirely.</p>"),
        ("Brushing the crossing point between the loungers and the ladder",
         "<p>The stretch of turf that gets crossed barefoot between a set of loungers and the pool ladder mats down faster than any other section of a {city} deck border, since it carries more repeated foot traffic in a single afternoon than the rest of the yard sees in a week. Brushing that crossing against the grain every few weeks keeps the blades standing instead of laying over permanently along the worn path. A section further from the water, along a side fence, rarely needs the same attention since almost nobody walks it directly.</p>"),
        ("Why does a glued seam need a different post-storm check than a nailed one?",
         "<p>After heavy wind or rain, a {city} lanai floor's glued seams deserve a closer look than a nailed perimeter would, since adhesive can lift quietly at a slab edge without the visible tenting a loose nail leaves behind. Running a hand along the seam line where it meets the screen frame catches a soft spot before it becomes a lifted corner that traps water underneath. A border with a nailed or bender-board edge shows problems more obviously, a raised corner anyone would notice on sight, so it needs the same check but rarely the same level of attention.</p>"),
        ("Watching infill near the splash zone specifically",
         "<p>Coated sand or zeolite infill along the section of a {city} border closest to the water depletes faster than infill further back, since regular hose rinsing and pool splash both wash a small amount out with every use, on top of whatever a summer storm contributes. Checking depth in that specific stretch every few months, rather than assuming the whole yard wears evenly, catches a thinning spot before it shows as a bare-looking patch in the exact area guests and family see most often. Topping off just that section is a smaller job than waiting for the whole border to look thin.</p>"),
        ("Keeping the deck drain clear inside a screen enclosure",
         "<p>A screen mesh keeps most large debris out of a {city} lanai, but pollen, dust, and small leaves still settle on the turf and can work toward the deck drain over time, especially during the June-to-September rainy season when storms are frequent enough that the drain rarely gets a rest. A quick sweep or rinse toward the drain on a regular schedule keeps the whole underlay system doing what it was built for, since even a permeable backing cannot help much once the drain it feeds is the part that is actually clogged.</p>"),
    ],
}
